WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:22.440
Friedrich Nietzsche was a nineteenth century
philosopher who is most remembered for his

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:25.750
killer quotes and incredibly large mustache.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:30.170
The main goal of his work was aimed to uncover
the fact that there is no universal truth,

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:36.000
and that the human condition is in a constant
state of overcoming our animalistic urges.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.970
He came up with many amazing quotes that we
still use to this very day, including “What

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:44.829
doesn’t kill me makes me stronger”, and
“God is dead”.

00:00:44.829 --> 00:00:49.420
While he is often misunderstood, and his work
has been used for the justification of evil

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:54.519
over the past hundred years, the philosophy
of Friedrich Nietzsche truly has changed the

00:00:54.519 --> 00:00:58.680
way people live in the 21st Century.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.063
Today, we’re going to talk about his life,
philosophy, and legacy.

00:01:00.063 --> 00:01:12.070
Early Life and Influences to His Philosophy
Friedrich Nietzsche was born on October 15th,

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:16.670
1844 in Saxony, Prussia, which is now apart
of modern-day Germany.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:20.740
He just so happened to be born on the same
birthday as King Friedrich Wilhelm IV, which

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:24.079
is why his parents decided to name him after
their ruler.

00:01:24.079 --> 00:01:26.770
He grew up in a very strict Protestant family.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:30.960
His father was a priest, and his grandfather
published books defending Lutheranism.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:36.421
His father contracted an excruciatingly painful
brain disease that lasted for a year before

00:01:36.421 --> 00:01:39.049
he died.

00:01:39.049 --> 00:01:42.880
And in 1849, Friedreich's younger brother,
Ludwig, died when he was only 2 years old.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:46.430
By the time he started going to Kindergarten,
Nietzsche had seen so much death and suffering

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:49.569
in his young life, he was already a very serious
person.

00:01:49.569 --> 00:01:54.929
He was nearsighted and got constant headaches
from a condition called myopia, so he already

00:01:54.929 --> 00:01:56.810
wore glasses as a young child.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:01.530
He looked and acted like a tiny professor
who was too grown-up for childish games.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:06.130
Obviously, he wasn’t very popular with his
classmates, and he didn’t make very many

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:07.130
friends.

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:09.190
Most of his childhood was spent surrounded
by women.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:13.560
His mother, grandmother, and two aunts all
shared in the responsibilities of raising

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:14.560
him.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:18.620
In 1956, Friedrich Nietzsche, his sister Elisabeth,
and his mother moved to their own house in

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:20.090
Naumburg, Germany.

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:24.120
That house would become the place where he
wrote his most important work, and the property

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.850
has been preserved today as a museum.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:30.750
Nietzsche attended a Christian prep school,
and later transferred to a boarding school.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:34.170
He never got particularly good grades, but
his father had a state job before he passed

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:37.689
away, which meant that he got a full scholarship
to any college of his choice.

00:02:37.689 --> 00:02:41.360
He chose to study theology and philosophy
at Bonn University.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.030
He originally wanted to become a priest, like
his father.

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:48.489
After just two semesters, he realized that
his personal beliefs were in direct conflict

00:02:48.489 --> 00:02:52.480
with the theological curriculum, and he often
got into arguments with his professors.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:58.170
He had to transfer to The University of Leipzig,
and he studied the ancient languages of Latin

00:02:58.170 --> 00:02:59.170
and Greek, instead.

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:03.470
As a student, Nietzsche contracted syphilis
from a prostitute at a brothel, which made

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:07.250
him physically weak and in constant pain for
the rest of his life.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:12.120
Syphilis is also known for causing people
to fall into madness, which did happen to

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:13.120
him, eventually.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:14.840
At that time, there wasn’t a cure for syphilis.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:20.820
Today, it’s treated with penicillin, but
that would not be invented until 1923, after

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:21.830
his death.

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:25.870
Nietzsche spent much of his free time listening
to live performances of orchestras and operas.

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:29.739
The music was so beautiful, it helped him
to forget about his pain.

00:03:29.739 --> 00:03:33.920
He was particularly fascinated by the work
of composer Richard Wagner, and he would pay

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:37.269
to see each new performance 3 or 4 times in
a row.

00:03:37.269 --> 00:03:42.300
He was completely inspired by the tragedy
of the characters in Wagner’s operas, and

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:44.450
he eventually got to meet the composer.

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:48.620
They became very good friends, and Richard
Wagner was like a father figure to Nietzsche.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:53.170
When he was 24 years old, he worked as a full-time
professor of languages at the University of

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:54.170
Basel.

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:58.650
He taught for several years, even though he
claimed that he was not all that happy in

00:03:58.650 --> 00:03:59.650
the classroom.

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:02.819
He decided to volunteer his time as an orderly
at the hospital after school.

00:04:02.819 --> 00:04:06.409
But he ended up contracting dysentery and
diphtheria from the patients.

00:04:06.409 --> 00:04:11.480
His health continued to decline so dramatically,
he would sometimes end up bedridden for several

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:12.480
days straight.

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:16.320
He chose to retire from being a professor
in his 30’s, and lived on a very modest

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:17.320
pension.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:21.140
For Nietzsche, simply staying alive was a
constant battle.

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:25.350
He spent so much time in bed with nothing
to do, so he was always thinking about the

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:27.340
meaning of life and humanity.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:33.050
This is when he began to write books on his
philosophy.

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:42.120
The Crux of Nietzsche’s Philosophy
Friedrich Nietzsche believed that there is

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:47.930
no absolute truth, and that people should
not have blind faith in their church or political

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:48.930
leaders.

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:52.210
Today, this sounds like common sense, but,
back in the 1800’s, people were raised that

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:58.000
there were absolute right and wrong answers,
and nothing in-between.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.790
By telling people to inquire rather than to
just blindly accept something, he inspired

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:06.449
future generations to completely change the
way we think.

00:05:06.449 --> 00:05:11.050
One of his most famous quotes, “God is Dead”
is used by Atheists everywhere, but it is

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:14.330
actually misunderstood, and taken out of context.

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:16.650
What he really said, was, “God is dead.

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:17.860
God remains dead.

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:19.400
And we have killed him.

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:23.800
How shall we comfort ourselves, the murderers
of all murderers?”

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:26.139
He wasn’t celebrating the death of God,
though.

00:05:26.139 --> 00:05:30.539
He was making a statement that more and more
people were losing faith all the time, and

00:05:30.539 --> 00:05:35.470
he believed that without Christianity, mankind
didn’t know how to figure out right and

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:36.470
wrong.

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:40.030
Without the existence of religion, people
often look up to their political leaders and

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:43.220
royal families with god-like reverence, instead.

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:48.039
He knew that people needed something else
to take its place.

00:05:48.039 --> 00:05:52.220
He wanted people to embrace the arts like
music, literature, and theater, instead, and

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:57.319
that Shakespeare had just as much value to
human morality as the Bible, without giving

00:05:57.319 --> 00:06:00.220
away their ability to think for themselves.

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:05.550
He believed that rules of society were developed
to keep people down, and maintain the status

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:06.550
quo.

00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:10.130
For example, in Christianity, it is taught
that people should be like Jesus Christ, and

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:13.099
not have a desire for material things.

00:06:13.099 --> 00:06:17.730
Nietzsche believes that this is only taught
to the poor to manipulate them to accept their

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:18.800
station in life.

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:23.789
If people are taught to feel shame for feeling
envious of others and desiring more, they

00:06:23.789 --> 00:06:26.230
may not try to achieve a higher status.

00:06:26.230 --> 00:06:30.199
And, therefore, if they’re happy with less,
they won’t cause a revolution against their

00:06:30.199 --> 00:06:33.180
government to demand more.

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:37.780
Nietzsche tells us to own our selfish desires,
and that the rules of society are something

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:42.330
that need to be overcome in order to get to
the real truth of our lives.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:46.880
Nietzsche believed that every individual person
should be in the process of “selbst uberwindung”

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:48.330
or “self-overcoming”.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:51.890
If we are envious of someone else, this is
just a sign of what we really need in order

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:53.410
to become our true selves.

00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:57.280
For example, if you feel envious of guys who
look muscular, it’s a sign you should go

00:06:57.280 --> 00:06:58.280
to the gym.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:02.630
If you’re jealous of someone who is rich,
you could find a job that makes more money...etcetera,

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:04.180
etcetera.

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:08.620
Everyone has their own dream, and if we refuse
to acknowledge these desires, we’re only

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:11.110
going to make ourselves miserable.

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:16.870
And even if we fail to achieve our goals,
at least we have the satisfaction of knowing

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:18.060
we tried.

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:22.090
Nietzsche said that if someone has succeeded
in overcoming obstacles while also accepting

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:29.919
their failures with grace, they have become
an “ubermensch” or “superman”.

00:07:29.919 --> 00:07:33.569
He claimed that every man should aspire to
become like this.

00:07:33.569 --> 00:07:38.810
He said that most people are stuck in-between
their animal instincts, and the “ubermensch”,

00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:42.139
and there is a constant struggle between the
two impulses.

00:07:42.139 --> 00:07:46.210
He was one of the first people to come up
with the concept that “ignorance is bliss”,

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:51.240
and that it’s easy to be happy as a human
if you just accept the status quo.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:55.240
He admits that the quest for knowledge and
to become the ubermensch might make him miserable

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:59.819
along the way, but it was a life he chose
to pursue, nonetheless.

00:07:59.819 --> 00:08:04.790
Many people have read his concept of “ubermensch”
and misinterpreted it as the ability to become

00:08:04.790 --> 00:08:07.379
someone who is superior to the rest of humanity.

00:08:07.379 --> 00:08:12.389
Others, like the Nazis, would see this as
proof that certain races were more civilized,

00:08:12.389 --> 00:08:16.319
and therefore, more human than others.

00:08:16.319 --> 00:09:41.390
Personal Life

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:45.600
Even though Friedrich Nietzsche is a household
name today, his work would not be translated

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:50.370
into other languages until the 1890’s, and
by that point, he was already too sick to

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:51.760
enjoy his new popularity.

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:56.550
So, while his philosophy was well-known in
Germany, he wasn’t exactly becoming rich

00:09:56.550 --> 00:09:57.550
and famous.

00:09:57.550 --> 00:10:01.950
In fact, his philosophy made him very unpopular,
and it ruined his friendship with Richard

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:05.829
Wagner, who thought he was going mad.

00:10:05.829 --> 00:10:09.140
Nietzsche decided to isolate himself in the
Swiss Alps, where he could spend a lot of

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:10.490
time in nature.

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:15.560
He often went on long walks thinking of nothing
but philosophy, only to go home and write

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:17.839
his new ideas for his next book.

00:10:17.839 --> 00:10:21.870
He wrote several volumes of his ideas, and
sometimes got letters in the mail from fellow

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:24.260
philosophers and people who wanted to be his
friend.

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:27.470
He met a psychoanalyst named Lou Andreas-Salomé.

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:32.190
When she read Nietzsche’s work, she felt
that his philosophy perfectly aligned with

00:10:32.190 --> 00:10:34.089
everything she had already been thinking.

00:10:34.089 --> 00:10:38.260
She was only 20 years old at that time, but
Nietzsche considered her to be his protege.

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:43.240
Lou was trying to challenge the norms of society
by experimenting to see if men and women can

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:46.880
truly be friends without sex getting in the
way.

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:50.960
In 1882, Nietzsche decided to go on a year-long
vacation with Italy with Lou Andreas-Salomé,

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:55.820
and their mutual friend, Paul Rée, who was
also a philosopher.

00:10:55.820 --> 00:10:59.620
The three friends wanted to buy an old monastery
and start an academic commune together so

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:04.240
that they could live in a society that was
only filled with other like-minded intellectuals.

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:09.010
Friedrich Nietzsche was excited, and hoped
that he could finally have a group of friends

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:12.709
who understood him, but his family was totally
against it.

00:11:12.709 --> 00:11:16.850
His sister, Elisabeth, was always writing
him letters telling him to stop trying to

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:17.850
make a cult.

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:22.100
There was a bit of a love triangle, because
Friedrich and Paul were both in love with

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:24.300
Lou, and they were competing to win her heart.

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:28.310
Paul proposed to her first, but she rejected
him, saying that she liked him enough for

00:11:28.310 --> 00:11:31.310
them to live together, but more like “brother
and sister”.

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:35.070
She claimed that sex got in the way of thinking
clearly, and she used Nietzsche’s own philosophy,

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:39.481
saying said that society demands that women
get married, but she personally wished to

00:11:39.481 --> 00:11:42.459
remain celibate and focus on her academic
work.

00:11:42.459 --> 00:11:46.519
Even though he agreed to be friends, Friedrich
Nietzsche was still only human, and the more

00:11:46.519 --> 00:11:49.100
he got to know her, the more he was falling
in love.

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:52.910
After all, Lou seemed to be the only woman
on the planet who completely understood his

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:53.910
life’s work.

00:11:53.910 --> 00:11:57.190
He proposed to her, but she put him in the
friend zone, just like Paul Ree.

00:11:57.190 --> 00:12:01.200
In her defense, Nietzsche did have syphilis,
and marrying him would have meant that she

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:03.070
would have contracted the disease, as well.

00:12:03.070 --> 00:12:06.339
But she continued to live with him- flirting,
teasing, and leading him on.

00:12:06.339 --> 00:12:10.560
He remained hopeful that she would eventually
come around, and he proposed to her two more

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:11.560
times.

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:15.630
No amount of intellectual reasoning could
overcome his human desire, and this situation

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:18.670
became way too uncomfortable for the three
of them.

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:22.770
His dream of starting a commune never happened,
and Lou and Paul rode away on their horse-drawn

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:24.880
carriage, leaving Friedrich behind.

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:28.220
It’s probably best that he got out of that
situation, though, because she later broke

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:32.139
up with Paul Ree and married a man named Friedrich
Carl Andreas.

00:12:32.139 --> 00:12:35.360
They apparently remained celibate throughout
the entire course of their marriage.

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:40.200
She actually did have sexual affairs, though,
which is just a bit hypocritical.

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:43.370
She also had an emotional affair with none
other than Sigmund Freud.

00:12:43.370 --> 00:12:48.380
It turns out that all of this was one big
experiment to her, and in 1910, she published

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:52.830
a book called The Erotic, where we learn that
she spent most of her life trying to analyze

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:57.779
and document the differences between men and
women when it came to sex.

00:12:57.779 --> 00:13:04.760
Nietzsche’s Final Days
The relationship with Lou Andreas-Salomé

00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:08.240
completely traumatized Friedrich Nietzsche,
and he never got married.

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:13.240
He often wrote about love, and how it can
make the most logical person feel like they’re

00:13:13.240 --> 00:13:14.240
going crazy.

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:18.170
Instead of writing about his own life, Nietzsche
expressed his feelings in a book called Thus

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:23.470
Spoke Zarathustra, about a man who isolated
himself in the mountains to find all of the

00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:28.170
wisdom in the world, but he could not find
any disciples to listen to him or follow him.

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:30.410
So, he becomes one of the most lonely men
in the world.

00:13:30.410 --> 00:13:35.300
Since he was rejecting Christianity, Nietzsche
knew that mankind needed some kind of moral

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:36.720
compass to live by.

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:41.740
So he wrote Beyond Good and Evil, and addressed
all of the issues of what makes something

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:43.779
good or bad.

00:13:43.779 --> 00:13:47.410
In 1888, he could feel his mind and body betraying
him.

00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:52.300
His last book was called The AntiChrist, where
he expressed his “transvaluation of all

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:55.990
values”, where he was trying to come up
with a set of rules for morality that could

00:13:55.990 --> 00:13:56.990
replace Christianity.

00:13:56.990 --> 00:14:01.870
He saw himself as a martyr who was making
a sacrifice for the human race.

00:14:01.870 --> 00:14:05.930
In 1889, he was only 44 years old, and he
had a nervous breakdown.

00:14:05.930 --> 00:14:09.330
He was triggered into hysteria when he was
walking in the street of Turin, Italy, and

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:12.149
he saw a man beating his horse with a whip.

00:14:12.149 --> 00:14:15.160
He ran to the horse, hugging its neck to protect
it.

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:17.860
He was sobbing, and saying, “I understand
you.”

00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:22.070
His landlord, David Fino, had to usher him
out of the street, and get him back to his

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:23.070
room.

00:14:23.070 --> 00:14:27.019
He spent the next two days laying on the couch
in a catatonic state, so Fino arranged for

00:14:27.019 --> 00:14:29.420
him to get home to Basel, Germany.

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:33.329
Even though they had a falling out, Richard
Wagner and his wife were still his true friends.

00:14:33.329 --> 00:14:36.440
They were the ones to help him get into a
mental hospital.

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:40.029
During one of the last moments where he was
responsive, he started to sketch a picture

00:14:40.029 --> 00:14:45.040
of a man and a woman hugging one another in
a long embrace, before he slipped into a permanent

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:46.320
catatonic state.

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:50.649
It was almost as if he was trying to say that
all of his philosophical work was nothing

00:14:50.649 --> 00:14:55.699
without someone to share it with, and that
at the end of his life, love was the one thing

00:14:55.699 --> 00:14:57.769
he truly wanted more than anything else.

00:14:57.769 --> 00:15:02.579
At the hospital, he was diagnosed with paralytic
dementia, which was a direct result of his

00:15:02.579 --> 00:15:05.839
battle with syphilis for over 20 years.

00:15:05.839 --> 00:15:09.860
But many people believe that the pressure
he put on himself to find the answer to morality

00:15:09.860 --> 00:15:14.780
without God, and to unravel the inner workings
of the human condition is what ultimately

00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:15.780
made him go crazy.

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:19.889
He slipped into madness, and was unresponsive
when people tried to speak to him.

00:15:19.889 --> 00:15:22.529
He eventually died in the year 1900.

00:15:22.529 --> 00:15:30.320
The Dangers of Studying Nietzsche
Soon after his death, Friedrich Nietzsche’s

00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:35.220
work was being translated into multiple languages,
and it began to inspire people around the

00:15:35.220 --> 00:15:36.220
world.

00:15:36.220 --> 00:15:39.000
However, many people would read his work and
completely misunderstand what he was trying

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:40.000
to say.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:44.360
Even his sister, Elisabeth, was a pseudo-Nazi,
and later re-published a second edition of

00:15:44.360 --> 00:15:45.360
his books.

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:49.380
She rewrote certain sections of his work to
glorify the German race as being superior

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:50.620
to the rest of the world.

00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:55.529
Because of this, Nietzsche has been blamed
for starting not just one, but two world wars.

00:15:55.529 --> 00:16:00.130
Before World War I, even priests were preaching
Nietzsche’s philosophy at their pulpits,

00:16:00.130 --> 00:16:02.080
relating it to their own countries.

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:05.510
This caused a lot of national pride, especially
in Germany.

00:16:05.510 --> 00:16:09.569
Adolf Hitler became a fan of Nietzsche, and
he took this as a sign that Germans were far

00:16:09.569 --> 00:16:11.560
more evolved than the rest of the human race.

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:15.740
During the rise of the Third Reich in the
1930’s, fascists often quoted Nietzsche,

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:18.520
especially with his idea of the “ubermensch”.

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:21.460
They wanted to make an entire race of the
supermen.

00:16:21.460 --> 00:16:24.750
Nietzsche’s sister welcomed Adolf Hitler
to the museum, where they posed together for

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:27.540
a photograph next to the bust of Friedrich
Nietzsche.

00:16:27.540 --> 00:16:31.149
Even today, many critics of Nietzsche’s
work consider his books to be too dangerous

00:16:31.149 --> 00:16:32.540
for young people to read.

00:16:32.540 --> 00:16:38.170
Since his main philosophy is to question everything,
this can often manifest in acts of rebellion

00:16:38.170 --> 00:16:39.170
in teenagers.

00:16:39.170 --> 00:16:42.610
After all, if they question their teachers,
parents, and government, they are more likely

00:16:42.610 --> 00:16:43.610
to lash out.

00:16:43.610 --> 00:16:47.089
In the 1924, this happened in the case of
Leopold and Loeb.

00:16:47.089 --> 00:16:49.560
They were two college students from Chicago
who thought that they were so much smarter

00:16:49.560 --> 00:16:53.570
than everyone else, and that they were the
example of the “ubermensch” Nietzsche

00:16:53.570 --> 00:16:54.680
was writing about.

00:16:54.680 --> 00:16:56.910
They believed that they could commit the “perfect
crime”.

00:16:56.910 --> 00:16:59.120
So, they murdered a 14-year old boy.

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:03.180
Their defense lawyer cited Nietzsche as the
reason why they committed murder.

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:08.100
Yes, this is a ridiculous excuse, because
plenty of people read Nietzsche, and they

00:17:08.100 --> 00:17:09.329
don’t become serial killers.

00:17:09.329 --> 00:17:14.260
But the jury actually bought it, and the young
men got away with murder, because they considered

00:17:14.260 --> 00:17:17.790
his philosophy be just that dangerous.

00:17:17.790 --> 00:17:23.150
Despite his criticisms, civilization of the
20th and 21st century were completely changed

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:26.690
by Nietzsche's teachings, and they continue
on to this day.

00:17:26.690 --> 00:17:30.960
Now, it is very normal to think critically
and question the world around us, and it is

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:33.080
often the basis of liberal arts education.

00:17:33.080 --> 00:17:37.820
And for those of us who do not go to church,
we tend to learn about right and wrong from

00:17:37.820 --> 00:17:40.170
what we see on TV and in the movies.

00:17:40.170 --> 00:17:44.100
Whether we realize it or not, we are living
in a very Nietzsche world.

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:57.500
It’s just a shame he didn’t live to see
it.

